      ],
      "icon": "structure"
    },
    {
      "id": "y7uytbop",
      "title": "Making the abstract concrete",
      "rows": [
        {
          "id": "eyopefy0",
          "layout": "2-1",
          "cells": [
            [
              {
                "id": "q3k6ts3v",
                "kind": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "text": "Show the arithmetic, do not explain the concept"
              },
              {
                "id": "7u1jxq7f",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "The instinct with an unfamiliar financial product is to explain it. We did the opposite and showed the sum, in the plainest numbers we could find:"
              },
              {
                "id": "kjisc86l",
                "kind": "table",
                "head": [
                  "",
                  ""
                ],
                "rows": [
                  [
                    "Property value",
                    "SAR 10,000,000"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Number of shares",
                    "10,000"
                  ],
                  [
                    "Price per share",
                    "SAR 1,000"
                  ],
                  [
                    "You invest SAR 10,000",
                    "You own 1% of the property, and 1% of its rent"
                  ]
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "rk9nr29p",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "One worked example does what three paragraphs of definition cannot. A reader who follows that division has understood fractional ownership without ever being taught the term."
              },
              {
                "id": "fx90tyml",
                "kind": "beforeAfter",
                "before": "/uploads/1784470684570-Screenshot-2026-07-19-at-5.17.45-PM.png",
                "after": "/uploads/1784470688740-Screenshot-2026-07-19-at-5.17.33-PM.png",
                "caption": "Drag to compare.",
                "ratio": "wide",
                "fit": "natural"
              }
            ],
            [
              {
                "id": "bej1m84z",
                "kind": "image",
                "src": "/uploads/1784470797295-screencapture-hissatech-sa-en-tokenizati.png",
                "alt": "",
                "caption": "Explain why this matters — not what it is.",
                "ratio": "wide",
                "fit": "natural",
                "framed": true,
                "size": "column"
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "icon": "layers"
    },
    {
      "id": "hjttf2a9",
      "title": "The calculator",
      "rows": [
        {
          "id": "05bwzz0b",
          "layout": "1",
          "cells": [
            [
              {
                "id": "icukcvfw",
                "kind": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "text": "Answering the question before it is asked"
              },
              {
                "id": "wt14k8p1",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "Every prospective investor arrives with the same question: what do I actually get back. The honest answer lives inside the app, behind a download. The calculator brings it forward onto the landing page, before anyone has committed to anything. You enter what you want to invest, and you see the monthly income and the expected profit if the property is sold in three to five years."
              },
              {
                "id": "4p8sif5z",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "It carries its own disclaimer, in plain language: the figures are estimates, they depend on the property and the rental market, and real results will differ. A calculator that overpromises does more damage than no calculator at all, especially inside a regulatory sandbox."
              },
              {
                "id": "gt13kc28",
                "kind": "image",
                "src": "/uploads/1784471481577-Group-13.png",
                "alt": "",
                "caption": "",
                "ratio": "wide",
                "fit": "natural",
                "framed": false,
                "size": "column"
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "icon": "price"
    },
    {
      "id": "6bvk8olv",
      "title": "Trust as a design material",
      "rows": [
        {
          "id": "hru8qjdp",
          "layout": "1",
          "cells": [
            [
              {
                "id": "6j7byrb0",
                "kind": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "text": "The credibility has to be on the page"
              },
              {
                "id": "gp7stt7v",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "For a product this unfamiliar, the trust signals are not decoration around the product. They are part of it, and each one is specific rather than generic:"
              },
              {
                "id": "i32h5lhk",
                "kind": "list",
                "items": [
                  "The Sharia ruling is named. Reviewed and approved by Sheikh Prof. Yousef bin Abdullah Al Shubaily, with the certificate itself one tap away.",
                  "The regulator is named. REGA sandbox, stated on the home page and explained in full on the about page.",
                  "The partners are named. Mada, Nafath, the Real Estate Authority, Arab National Bank, Lean.",
                  "The founders are named, with photographs and real biographies."
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "fswbt2p7",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "A badge that says \"secure\" persuades nobody. A scholar's name, a regulator's programme, and a bank you already use are checkable, and checkable is the only kind of trust that survives a serious investor."
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "icon": "insight"
    },
    {
      "id": "gnfnn76o",
      "title": "Arabic first",
      "rows": [
        {
          "id": "njd2b8vt",
          "layout": "1",
          "cells": [
            [
              {
                "id": "j6waew90",
                "kind": "heading",
                "level": 2,
                "text": "Built right to left, not flipped"
              },
              {
                "id": "leme7beq",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": "The product is Arabic first and reads right to left throughout: layout, navigation, forms, numerals, and the direction of every progress sequence. The English version is the mirror, not the original."
              },
              {
                "id": "5mozrgma",
                "kind": "paragraph",
                "text": " The copy is Saudi, not Modern Standard Arabic translated from English. Lines like \"تقدر تشوف العوائد قبل ما تختار\" are how the audience actually speaks, and in a market where most fintech copy reads as translated, that is a trust signal of its own."
              },
              {
                "id": "noiqa6ly",
                "kind": "image",
                "src": "/uploads/1784471889006-Group-15.png",
                "alt": "",
                "caption": "",
                "ratio": "wide",
                "fit": "natural",
                "framed": false,
                "size": "column"
              }
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "icon": "design"
    }
  ],
